course: euphemisms
lesson: 26
title: Soft words for hard things: war, the military and violence
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: War means killing and destruction, and the people who start or run wars often do not want to say that plainly.
explain: So armies and governments invent cool, technical-sounding words that hide the blood: an attack becomes "kinetic action", a retreat becomes a "tactical redeployment".
explain: These words are mostly spin and hiding, not kindness: they make terrible things sound tidy, careful or even normal.
explain: A vivid example: "We degraded the enemy's capability" can mean "we bombed them and many people died".
explain: When you hear calm, official words about fighting, ask: who got hurt, and what really happened?
fact: "Pacification" means violently crushing the people of a place until they stop resisting.
fact: "Surgical strike" means a bombing that is claimed to hit only its target, though it often hits more.
fact: "Soft target" means unprotected people or places, often ordinary civilians (people who are not soldiers).
fact: "Servicing the target" is US military jargon for bombing or killing the target.
fact: "Kinetic action" is US military jargon for a real attack with weapons that kills or destroys.
fact: "Degrade" in war talk means damage or destroy, often killing many people.
fact: "Losses" in war reports means soldiers killed.
fact: "Special military operation" is an official name used to avoid the word war.
fact: "Police action" is an old US official name for a war, such as the Korean War.
fact: "Conflict" is the official, softer word for a war.
fact: "Theatre of operations" is military jargon for a war zone.
fact: "Strategic withdrawal" is the official way to say an army retreated.
fact: "Tactical redeployment" is military jargon for a retreat.
fact: "Pre-emptive strike" means attacking first, before the enemy attacks you.
fact: "Ethnic cleansing" means driving out or killing a whole ethnic group (a people who share family roots, language or customs) from an area.
fact: "The final solution" was the name used by the Nazis (the National Socialists, who ruled Germany from 1933 to 1945) for their plan to murder all the Jews of Europe.
fact: "The deterrent" is the British official way to say nuclear weapons, the most destructive bombs ever made.
fact: "Battle fatigue" is an old name for the mental injury soldiers get from combat, now called trauma.
fact: "Private military contractor" is the official name for a hired soldier, a mercenary.
fact: "The Troubles" is the everyday Irish name for the violent conflict in Northern Ireland from the 1960s to 1998.
exercise: What does "pacification" of a region really mean? | Violently crushing the people until they stop resisting | Bringing peace talks and gifts to the people of the region | Teaching the people to swim in the Pacific | Calming the people down with music
exercise: What is a "surgical strike"? | A bombing claimed to hit only its target | An operation done by army doctors in a tent | A strike by hospital workers | A sharp cut with a knife in a fight
exercise: In war talk, what is a "soft target"? | Unprotected people, often civilians | A target made of cushions for practice | An enemy who gives up easily | A target that is easy to miss
exercise: What does "servicing the target" mean to a bomber crew? | Bombing or killing the target | Repairing the target after a battle | Bringing food to the target | Checking the target's engine
exercise: What is "kinetic action" when a general says it? | A real attack with weapons | A fitness drill for soldiers | Moving soldiers by train | A science lesson on motion
exercise: An army says it will "degrade" the enemy. What does it mean? | Damage or destroy them, killing many | Give them lower marks on their army tests | Take away their medals | Make their uniforms look shabby
exercise: A war report says "our losses were heavy". What does "losses" mean? | Soldiers killed | Money spent on fuel | Games the army lost | Maps and tools left behind
exercise: What is a "special military operation" really? | A war | A training exercise | A rescue after a flood | A secret parade
exercise: What was the "police action" in Korea? | A war | A police training camp | A traffic patrol | A search for a thief
exercise: When news calls a fight between two countries a "conflict", what is it usually? | A war | A quarrel between two leaders | A sports match | A court case
exercise: What is a "theatre of operations"? | A war zone | A hospital operating room | A cinema for soldiers | A stage where plays are shown
exercise: What does "strategic withdrawal" mean? | Retreat | Taking money from a bank | Sending a clever spy | Moving forward to win
exercise: What is a "tactical redeployment" by an army? | A retreat | A new attack | A holiday for the troops | A change of uniform
exercise: What is a "pre-emptive strike"? | Attacking first before the enemy does | A strike by workers before payday | Striking back after being attacked | A warning shot into the air
exercise: What does "ethnic cleansing" mean? | Driving out or killing a whole ethnic group | Washing the streets of a city with big hoses | Cleaning old buildings of a people | Teaching people about hygiene
exercise: What was "the final solution" of the Nazis? | Their plan to murder all the Jews of Europe | The last answer in a war exam | The peace treaty that ended the war in Europe | Their plan to rebuild Germany
exercise: When a British politician talks about "the deterrent", what are they talking about? | Nuclear weapons | A guard dog at the border | A warning sign | A police force
exercise: What was "battle fatigue"? | Mental injury from combat | Being sleepy after a long march | Tired muscles after training | Boredom between battles
exercise: What is a "private military contractor"? | A hired soldier, a mercenary | A builder for army houses | A soldier on leave | A company selling army food
exercise: In Ireland, what are "the Troubles"? | The violent conflict in Northern Ireland, 1960s to 1998 | Small problems in daily life, like a flat tyre or rain | A famous Irish band | The potato famine of the 1840s
test: News: "The ministry confirms our losses in the eastern sector were light." What really happened? | Some of their soldiers were killed, but not many | They lost a few maps and tools | They spent little money | They lost a football match in the eastern part of the country
test: A general says: "We carried out kinetic action against the depot overnight." What happened? | They attacked the depot with weapons | They moved the depot by lorry | They held a sports day at the depot | They inspected the depot quietly
test: "Our forces made a tactical redeployment to the river line." Which is true? | The army retreated to the river | The army won a big battle | The army went swimming | The army got new weapons
test: Grandad: "My uncle came back from the war with battle fatigue, never the same after." What was wrong? | He had a mental injury from combat | He just needed a good long sleep in his own bed | He had a broken leg | He was bored of marching
test: A leader calls the invasion of a neighbour a "special military operation". Why use that phrase? | To avoid admitting it is a war | Because it is a small parade | Because it is a medical operation | Because it is a training game
test: Mate: "lol my brother says a surgical strike means the army docs go on strike, right?" Is he right? | No, it is a bombing claimed to hit only its target | Yes, the army doctors stop working | Yes, it is a strike at a hospital | No, it is a punch in a boxing match
test: History book: "The village was pacified in 1902." What most likely happened there? | Its people were crushed by force until they stopped resisting | The villagers held a big peace party with music and dancing | The village was moved to the seaside | The village voted for a new mayor
test: A report says the rebels chose "soft targets" like markets. What does it mean? | They attacked unprotected ordinary people | They attacked pillow shops | They attacked well-guarded army bases | They picked targets that were easy to miss
test: "Bloody hell, they're calling a three-year war a 'conflict' now." What is the speaker angry about? | A war is being given a softer name | A conflict is worse than a war | There was no fighting at all | The war only lasted three days
test: A company boss: "We provide private military contractors abroad." What does the company sell? | Hired soldiers | Army food | Building work | Holidays for soldiers
test: An Irish nan says: "That was during the Troubles, love." What time is she talking about? | The violent years in Northern Ireland, 1960s to 1998 | A week when the car broke down and the roof leaked badly | The years of the potato famine | The time she had a cold
test: A minister: "The deterrent keeps Britain safe." What is he talking about? | Britain's nuclear weapons | A big fence on the coast | A team of police dogs | Road safety signs
test: "We'll degrade their air defences," says the spokesman. What will really happen? | The defences will be bombed and destroyed | The defences will be given worse marks | The defences will be painted grey | The defences will be bought
test: "They didn't run away — it was a strategic withdrawal!" says a boastful soldier. What really happened? | They retreated | They attacked bravely | They held their ground | They took a holiday
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression "ethnic cleansing"? | It denotes the expulsion or killing of an entire ethnic group from a territory | It denotes the sanitation of public streets and squares by municipal authorities | It denotes the restoration of ancient buildings | It denotes instruction in personal hygiene
exam: Identify the meaning of "the final solution" as employed by the National Socialist regime. | The plan to murder all the Jews of Europe | The concluding treaty of the war | The final examination of officers | The reconstruction of German cities
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established meaning of a "pre-emptive strike". | An attack launched before the adversary has attacked | A retaliation subsequent to an attack by the adversary | A cessation of labour prior to payment | A warning shot discharged into the air
exam: It is given that a commander reports that a target was "serviced". Ascertain what occurred. | The target was bombed or destroyed | The target was repaired | The target was supplied with provisions | The target was inspected
